HEVCTest - the Raspberry Pi 4's HEVC block, from RISC OS
=======================================================

The Pi 4 has an HEVC (H.265) decoder block of its own, separate from the
VideoCore's H.264 decoder. Linux drives it with Raspberry Pi's "rpivid"
driver. hevcdec is that driver's command builder (rpivid_h265.c, as it is)
around a small RISC OS layer: the block's registers mapped and used in
SVC mode, its clock at its maximum, contiguous memory from Physical
Memory Pools, and the two phases' completion polled.

The block is a stateless decoder: it's given each picture's parameters
and slices, already parsed. For this test they were parsed on a Linux
machine by FFmpeg's HEVC decoder and saved with the slices in each clip's
"trace", with checksums of the picture FFmpeg decoded. HEVCTest gives
the block every picture as the trace says and checks the block's picture
against FFmpeg's.

The clips (in clips; each has its MP4 file, mp4, and its trace):
  small   352x288, 20 pictures, B frames, wavefront (WPP) entry points
  slices  416x240, 12 pictures, four slices a picture
  nowpp   416x240, 12 pictures, no WPP, scaling lists
  odd     426x240, 8 pictures (coded 432 wide, cropped)
  hd      1920x1080, 60 pictures, B frames, WPP

Running it (please send back Result, and ResultT if Test is OK):
  Test     Every clip, every picture checked. Writes Result.
  Speed    The 1080p clip without checks: pictures a second. Writes
           ResultT.
  Verbose  The first three pictures of the small clip with the
           decoder's log, if Test fails. Writes ResultV.

This is the first time the block decodes anything from RISC OS. If a
run says a phase "didn't finish", restart before running anything else;
if the machine locks up, switch it off and on again.

Needs: a Raspberry Pi 4 (or 400) with RISC OS 5.23 or later (Physical
Memory Pools). 8-bit 4:2:0 only for now.
Part of riscos-reelhwaccel; GPL version 2 or later. hevcdec includes
Raspberry Pi's rpivid_h265.c (GPL version 2 or later; Copyright (C) 2020
Raspberry Pi (Trading) Ltd, based on the Cedrus VPU driver). The clips
are made from FFmpeg's testsrc2 pattern.
